    "findings": [
      "At normal yard size the crow remains a recognizable same-style black corvid with similar body, bill, tail volume and quiet detailed feather clusters. The fixed whole-canvas sampler retains the native framing; no conspicuous new body-scale pop is apparent between these two static reference/new views.",
      "Both feet show a leftward emerging hooked projection opposed in screen direction to a downward/rightward curved toe group. In the new source the far foot is more visibly raised/curled and the two opposing silhouettes are easier to separate than in the reference. The curl is genuine native source drawing, not a marker or translated foot layer.",
      "This reads as a grasp-ready or suspended curled-foot static study without a branch. It does not independently demonstrate weight-bearing perching. The feet are curved around empty space, and their visible endpoints/underfeet are at different image heights. A future real support profile must be tested rather than assumed.",
      "At192 output/~186CSS several adjacent front digits merge into small dark clusters. Enlarged native art suggests multiple front claws and rearward hooks, but exact anatomical ownership, all digit counts, and rear hallux identity are not reliably certified by this single2D view.",
      "The region masks mark overlapping screen-space hypotheses. They do not trace hidden toe paths, prove a wrap around a cylinder, or create front/back occlusion against an actual branch.",
      "Minor asymmetric claw lengths, dark overlapping toe clusters and native feather/body redraw remain. They are acceptable for this bounded static anatomy study; stronger physical support/occlusion or complete perching behavior remains held."
    ]
  },
